---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { wildlifeReviewedAt, wildlifeSources, wildlifeDistanceGroups, wildlifeContacts, wildlifeSafetyFaqs } from '../data/wildlifeSafety';
const faqSchema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: wildlifeSafetyFaqs.map(({ question, answer }) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: question, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: answer } })) };
---

<BaseLayout title="Banff Wildlife Safety: Bears, Elk, Cougars & Emergency Contacts" description="Prepare for Banff wildlife encounters with Parks Canada viewing distances, behaviour-based bear advice, elk and cougar guidance, and separate emergency and reporting contacts." lastUpdated={wildlifeReviewedAt}>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />
  <div class="container"><Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Wildlife', href: '/wildlife' }, { label: 'Wildlife Safety' }]} /></div>
  <header class="safety-hero"><div class="container">
    <span class="badge">Prepare before you go</span>
    <h1>Wildlife safety in Banff</h1>
    <p>Give animals room, avoid a close encounter, and learn the response before you need it. Town paths, picnic areas and roadside stops are wildlife habitat too.</p>
    <p class="review">Official guidance reviewed <time datetime={wildlifeReviewedAt}>29 October 2026</time>. This is preparation guidance, not a current wildlife or trail report.</p>
  </div></header>

  <div class="container safety-content">
    <section aria-labelledby="contacts-heading" class="contacts-section">
      <h2 id="contacts-heading">Emergency help and wildlife reports</h2>
      <div class="cards contacts">{Object.values(wildlifeContacts).map(contact => <article class="card">
        <h3>{contact.label}</h3><p class="phone"><a href={contact.href}>{contact.phone}</a></p><p>{contact.note}</p><a href={contact.source}>Official contact guidance</a>
      </article>)}</div>
      <p>Save these numbers and your route before leaving. Tell a trusted person when to expect you back. A phone page cannot provide emergency assistance, and cell coverage cannot be assumed. <a href={wildlifeSources.safety}>Read Parks Canada’s trip-safety advice</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <nav aria-label="On this page" class="jump-links"><a href="#space">Viewing distances</a><a href="#bears">Bears</a><a href="#elk">Elk</a><a href="#carnivores">Cougars, wolves & coyotes</a><a href="#preparation">Before the outing</a></nav>

    <section id="space" aria-labelledby="space-heading">
      <h2 id="space-heading">Leave space before taking a photograph</h2>
      <div class="cards">{wildlifeDistanceGroups.map(group => <article class="card distance"><strong>{group.metres} metres minimum</strong><h3>{group.species.join(', ')}</h3><a href={group.source}>Parks Canada viewing guidance</a></article>)}</div>
      <p>These are minimum viewing distances, not a protective boundary. Back off if an animal becomes alert, nervous or changes what it is doing because of you. Use binoculars or a long lens; never crowd, follow, surround, feed or lure wildlife.</p>
      <p>At the roadside, slow down and stay in your vehicle. Stop only where safe and legal, keep the visit brief and move on if there is a traffic jam. Do not block an animal’s path or get out for a closer view. <a href={wildlifeSources.viewing}>Read the complete viewing and roadside rules</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="bears" aria-labelledby="bear-heading">
      <h2 id="bear-heading">Bears: the encounter and behaviour matter</h2>
      <p>Parks Canada’s response guidance applies to both grizzly and black bears. Do not choose “play dead” or “fight back” from colour or species alone. <a href={wildlifeSources.bears}>Read the full official bear-encounter guide</a> before a trip; behaviour can be difficult to interpret.</p>
      <div class="cards">
        <article class="card"><h3>A bear has not noticed you</h3><p>Stop, remain calm and prepare your spray. Leave quietly without attracting its attention. Give it room; do not approach to identify or photograph it.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>A bear knows you are there</h3><p>Stay together, pick up small children, speak calmly and firmly, and back away slowly when possible. Do not run. Keep your backpack on and leave the bear an escape route.</p></article>
      </div>
      <article class="response"><h3>A defensive bear approaches</h3><p>A surprised bear, or one defending food or young, may be stressed. Remain calm and non-threatening; back away when it stops advancing. If it keeps approaching, stand your ground and prepare to use bear spray according to the product instructions.</p><p><strong>If defensive contact occurs:</strong> Parks Canada advises lying face down, legs apart, hands protecting the back of your neck and backpack on. Remain still until the bear leaves. If the attack continues, it may have become predatory: fight back.</p></article>
      <article class="response"><h3>A bear follows, stalks or attacks as a predator</h3><p>A non-defensive approach can have different motivations. If the bear follows you, stop, stand your ground, speak firmly and act aggressively to discourage it; use spray if it approaches within your canister’s effective range. In a predatory attack, including an attack at night, <strong>do not play dead</strong>. Try to reach a secure vehicle or building; if you cannot escape, fight back.</p></article>
      <p><a href="/bear-spray">Arrange bear spray and instruction</a>. Wind, cold, rain and product condition can affect performance. Carrying spray does not replace avoiding an encounter.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="elk" aria-labelledby="elk-heading">
      <h2 id="elk-heading">Elk: change your route when they need space</h2>
      <p>Keep at least 30 metres away, including on lawns and paths in town. Elk can strike with hooves or charge with antlers. Never walk through a group or between a cow and calf. If an elk becomes nervous, grinds its teeth or moves its ears back, increase the distance and leave it room.</p>
      <p>Be particularly cautious during spring calving and fall mating. Follow temporary closures; do not use a calendar end date as permission to approach. If your path is blocked, wait well back or choose another route instead of squeezing past. <a href={wildlifeSources.elk}>Parks Canada elk safety</a> and <a href={wildlifeSources.bulletins}>current advisories</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="carnivores" aria-labelledby="carnivore-heading">
      <h2 id="carnivore-heading">Cougars, wolves and coyotes</h2>
      <p>Leave at least 100 metres when observing. If one approaches without fear, Parks Canada advises standing your ground, making yourself large and acting aggressively: shout, stomp or throw an object. Pick up small children, stay together, do not run and do not jump towards the animal. Be ready to use bear spray. <a href={wildlifeSources.carnivores}>Official carnivore encounter guidance</a>.</p>
      <p><strong>If a cougar attacks, fight back.</strong> Alberta advises using everything available, aiming at the face and eyes, getting back to your feet if knocked down and never playing dead. <a href={wildlifeSources.cougars}>Read Alberta’s cougar-specific response</a>.</p>
      <p>Once safe, report encounters to Park Dispatch at <a href={wildlifeContacts.wildlifeReport.href}>403-762-1470</a>. Note the location, animal description, behaviour and possible attractants; do not follow the animal to improve the report.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="preparation" aria-labelledby="prep-heading">
      <h2 id="prep-heading">Before a walk, ride, run or picnic</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Check <a href={wildlifeSources.bulletins}>Parks Canada warnings and closures</a> and the specific route. A nearby sighting does not establish whether a trail is open.</li>
        <li>Travel together, keep children close and make human voices heard near blind corners, running water and dense vegetation. Bear bells alone are not enough.</li>
        <li>Keep dogs leashed and under physical control; check restrictions and consider leaving a dog at home. Dogs can provoke an encounter.</li>
        <li>Keep food, rubbish and scented items controlled. Leave a large animal carcass immediately and report it.</li>
        <li>Carry bear spray where you can reach it on the trail, know the exact canister, and plan your emergency communication.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These preparation points follow <a href={wildlifeSources.bears}>Parks Canada’s bear-country guidance</a>. Winter also requires preparation: bears can wake briefly while denning, and other wildlife remains active. Never follow tracks towards an animal. <a href={wildlifeSources.winter}>Official winter safety advice</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="faq-heading"><h2 id="faq-heading">Common questions</h2>{wildlifeSafetyFaqs.map(faq => <details><summary>{faq.question}</summary><p>{faq.answer}</p></details>)}</section>
    <aside class="next"><h2>Build the rest of the outing</h2><p><a href="/wildlife-calendar">Seasonal wildlife preparation</a> · <a href="/wildlife">Wildlife field guide</a> · <a href="/trails">Trail planning</a> · <a href="/winter">Winter trip planning</a></p><p>Official source review covers Banff National Park and the linked Alberta cougar guidance. Other parks and carriers can have different rules. Recheck local signs and current notices on the day.</p></aside>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .safety-hero{background:var(--color-primary);color:white;padding:3rem 0}.safety-hero h1{color:white;max-width:850px}.safety-hero p{color:white;max-width:800px}.review{font-size:.9rem;opacity:.9}.safety-content{max-width:1080px;padding-top:2rem;padding-bottom:3rem}.safety-content section{margin:2.5rem 0;scroll-margin-top:7rem}.cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,270px),1fr));gap:1rem;margin:1.25rem 0}.card,.response,.next{padding:1.4rem;border:1px solid #d8d8ce;border-radius:12px;background:white}.contacts .card{border-top:4px solid var(--color-accent)}.phone{font-size:1.6rem;font-weight:750}.distance strong{font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--color-primary)}.response{margin:1rem 0;border-left:5px solid var(--color-accent)}.jump-links{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.75rem;background:var(--color-bg-alt);padding:1rem;border-radius:8px}.jump-links a{padding:.35rem}.safety-content a{text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.16em}.safety-content li{margin:.6rem 0}.safety-content details{padding:1rem;border-bottom:1px solid #d8d8ce}.safety-content summary{font-weight:700;cursor:pointer}.next{margin-top:2rem;background:var(--color-bg-alt)}@media print{.safety-hero{background:white;color:black;padding:1rem 0}.safety-hero h1,.safety-hero p{color:black}.jump-links{display:none}.card,.response{break-inside:avoid}}
</style>
